From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:02:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/efi: Reset the setup options on RTC power loss

With the UEFI variable store backend, the setup options are EFI
variables of the coreboot options GUID in SMMSTORE. A CMOS clear, the
usual way back to the defaults when the setup cannot be reached, then
resets the CMOS bytes and the date but none of the options.

efi_fv_delete_options() marks every live variable of a GUID as deleted
in place, clearing one more bit of its state byte as efi_fv_set_option()
does with the old copy: it needs no free space and no erase, and the
payload's reclaim drops the records later. It writes nothing unless the
whole store is well formed up to its erased tail, and nothing while
EDK2's fault tolerant write has an interrupted write of the store that
it will redo from the spare copy (the FTW working block after the
store), which would bring the variables back. An erased region, as
after a full flash, or an empty store is a success with nothing
deleted. The deletion walks from a copy of the store rdev, as the check
walk moves it to the end.

With DRIVERS_EFI_RESET_OPTIONS_ON_RTC_FAILURE, Cannon Lake romstage
calls it through efi_option_reset_all() when the power state snapshot
has RTC_PWR_STS, outside S3 and before FSP-M reads the first memory
option; only the console options are read earlier, so the console of
that boot keeps their old values up to ramstage. EDK2's CFR setup
creates each missing option with its default at the next start. Every
option goes back to its default, BIOS Lock, Intel ME and Firmware
protection included. Boot entries, the Secure Boot keys and the setup
password have other GUIDs and are kept, but an entry that a setup
switch removes, such as iPXE or CSMWrap, comes back if the switch was
off. For the rest of romstage get_uint_option() returns the fallbacks,
as the flash window may still be cached with the old records; ramstage
starts with the caches invalidated. That check is compiled only in
romstage with the option, so the bootblock, ramstage and SMM are
unchanged.

Nothing in coreboot clears RTC_PWR_STS on Cannon Lake, and the options
would then be reset at every boot. Romstage records a successful reset
in the power state before FSP-M, whose CBMEM hook copies it, and the
PMC final op of ramstage clears the bit only then, after FSP-S,
rtc_init() and the ELOG RTC reset event, as Tiger Lake clears it in
ramstage. A reset anywhere before that point (FSP-M, FSP-S, the ME)
repeats the boot with the bit still set: the deletion finds nothing
more, and the CMOS defaults and the ELOG event are not lost. FSP-S also
still sees the bit, as without this option. A refused store leaves the
bit set for the next boot to try again, unless FSP-M clears it itself
(Tiger Lake's seems to). The register is read back and logged.

TEST=tests/drivers/efivars.c: five new tests (only the records of the
GUID change, 0x3f to 0x3d and 0x3e to 0x3c, and a deletion that walks
from the moved rdev fails; malformed store refused without writes;
erased and empty stores; pending FTW write; write error), built on the
host with a cmocka stand-in, 30 of 30 pass. On a copy of the M920x
SMMSTORE (ci-151 dump): 31 variables deleted, their 31 state bytes the
only change, the 36 others kept; the erased SMMSTORE of the release
image is left as it is. The changed files pass a host syntax check for
every stage with the option on and off. On the running M920x the log
has "RTC Init", then the ELOG events, then "PCI: 00:00:1f.2 final", the
order the clear relies on. Not built with the coreboot toolchain.

For the hardware test, with the option on and GEN_PMCON_B bit 2 clear
before installing: after a CMOS clear, "Setup options: N reset to
defaults (RTC power lost)" in romstage, no "CMOS: me_state" line in
ramstage, "RTC: power failure cleared" with bit 2 clear after the ELOG
events, an ELOG RTC Reset event, the setup at its defaults and the
Secure Boot keys unchanged; efibootmgr unchanged only if iPXE and
CSMWrap were on before, otherwise their entries come back. Extra resets
from FSP or the ME on that boot are allowed. At the next boot, no
"reset to defaults" line and a "CMOS: me_state" line again. Firmware
protection is on again afterwards: turn it off before an internal
flashrom write.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
